 ## Background
-The techniques originate from the need to render complex, dynamic volumetric phenomena (smoke, clouds) and interactive deformations that cannot be efficiently stored in static voxel grids [G]. Procedural noise methods leverage the parallelism of GPUs to compute density analytically [8240-procedural-volumetric-cloud-rendering-gpu-shader.md]. Lens techniques extend standard projection models to support interactive exploration [6767-gpu-accelerated-ray-casting-with-volumetric-lenses.md]. Displacement mapping adapts surface modeling concepts to volume data for interactive editing [10659-displacement-mapping-for-volume-rendering.md]. Multi-frame rate rendering addresses the performance bottlenecks of high-resolution raycasting in distributed or multi-GPU environments [11338-dynamic-multi-frame-rate-rendering.md].
